{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.pomona.edu/administration/academic-dean/faculty-jobs#assistant-professor-of-biology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Biology",
      "description": "The Department of Biology is hiring a tenure track assistant professor of biology to join the department in July 2027. We are seeking a plant biologist in any area of plant biology, however those with an interest in plant physiology are especially encouraged to apply. We are looking for candidates with a demonstrated interest in teaching and conducting research with undergraduate students. The successful candidate will offer an upper-level course in Plant Physiology or other upper-level plant biology course with laboratory, and teach an introductory course with laboratory. Pomona College is a private, undergraduate, selective liberal arts institution enrolling approximately 1,700 students. Pomona College is the oldest and founding member of the Claremont Colleges, a consortium of institutions that include five undergraduate colleges and two graduate universities, located in southern California. Intercollegiate collaboration among the colleges provides university-scale services and facilities, including the Bernard Field Station and the Claremont Colleges Libraries. While each institution is independent, undergraduate students can take courses at any of the colleges and research collaboration among faculty is encouraged through professional development networks. Pomona faculty in the Environmental Analysis Department partner regularly with colleagues in and beyond Claremont. The faculty in Biology welcome applicants who are committed to high quality, innovative undergraduate teaching and to mentoring undergraduate student research. Pomona College’s plant growth facility includes greenhouses, growth chambers, and a headhouse for planting, preparation, and storage, with a full-time dedicated greenhouse manager (more detailed information on the facilities is available). The College actively promotes a dynamic and inclusive environment in which students and employees of diverse backgrounds, cultures, and perspectives can learn and work. Pomona College is an equal opportunity employer that welcomes members of all groups and reaffirms its commitment not to discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, gender, age, religion, caste, gender identity or expression, disability, or sexual orientation in its educational programs, activities, and employment practices. In recent years, over 51% of entering classes identified as students of color, 23% as first-generation, 14% international, and nearly 20% as Pell eligible students. Recruiting and retaining a diverse faculty committed to building an inclusive community is vital to educating an equally diverse student body. Candidates who can contribute to this goal are particularly encouraged to apply. Preferred Qualifications : A Ph.D. in a field of biology by the start of employment and expertise in plant biology is required. Teaching experience; a demonstrated commitment to undergraduate teaching and research; and a commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, in biology are also priorities. Minimum Qualifications : Ph.D. in a field of biology by the start of employment and expertise in plant biology. Application Deadline: (at 11:59 PM Pacific Time): 10/01/2026 Open Until Filled: Yes Special Instructions to Applicants : Applicants should submit to Academic Jobs Online #32243 1) a cover letter; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) a statement demonstrating commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, in environmental science; 4) a statement of teaching principles (3 pages maximum); 5) a research statement (3 pages maximum); and 6) graduate transcripts. As part of the application process, all candidates must provide contact information for at least three references who can submit a confidential letter of recommendation; however, only the references of short-listed candidates (those invited to a phone/Zoom interview) will be asked to submit a letter. All application materials should be posted by Thursday, October 1 at 11:59 pm PDT. You must meet the minimum qualifications to be considered for this position. Pomona College offers robust compensation. The salary for this position will range from $105,000 to $130,000 and will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training For any questions, please contact Dr. E. J. Crane, ej.crane@pomona.edu . When will references be contacted to provide a letter of recommendation? When candidate is invited to phone interview."
    },
    "https://www.pomona.edu/administration/academic-dean/faculty-jobs#assistant-professor-of-computer-science": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of  Computer Science",
      "description": "Pomona College seeks applications for an Assistant Professor of Computer Science to begin on July 1, 2027. We seek candidates with a strong commitment to i) inclusive undergraduate teaching across all levels of our curriculum; ii) mentoring a diverse student body and fostering a supportive learning environment; iii) developing a strong, independent research program that involves undergraduates and leads to peer-reviewed publications; and iv) helping build community within our Department and the College. We welcome applicants with broad intellectual interests from a wide range of subdisciplines, with a preference for candidates whose research and teaching interests complement those of the current faculty ( Computer Science Department Website ) or who can contribute to expanding the department's curricular range and scholarly interests. The teaching commitment is four courses per year over two semesters of instruction (2-2). CS faculty are expected to teach at least two required courses among our introductory (introduction to programming and computer science, mathematical foundations, and data structures) and/or core (algorithms, languages and theory of computation, and computer systems) sequences. Candidates should have a Ph.D. in hand by their start date, usually July 1, 2027. Our department values experience working with undergraduate students from diverse backgrounds and a commitment to improving higher education for underrepresented students through teaching, scholarship, or service. After graduation, recent CS majors have taken jobs in industry and national research labs and have pursued graduate programs at top research labs. Pomona College, located in Southern California, is the founding member of the Claremont Colleges, which also includes Claremont McKenna, Scripps, Pitzer, Harvey Mudd, and Claremont Graduate University. Students can cross-register for courses at any of the colleges, which allows Pomona students to enjoy the advantages of personalized instruction and close faculty-student interaction combined with the intellectual and extracurricular resources typically found at much larger universities. Pomona's Computer Science department currently has 10 full-time, tenured or tenure-track faculty members, and there are over 30 CS faculty across the Claremont Colleges. In collaboration with the Claremont College Consortium, Pomona College offers a variety of professional development, mentorship, and networking opportunities for junior faculty, including the opportunity for entering faculty members to apply for a fully funded leave in their fourth year at the College. A complete application includes a cover letter, a detailed curriculum vitae, graduate transcripts, three brief statements–one addressing the candidate’s teaching philosophy, one addressing the candidate’s scholarship, and one addressing the candidate’s ability to mentor a diverse student body , and three or more letters of reference, at least one of which evaluates the candidate's teaching. Please upload electronic copies of all materials to Academic Jobs Online #32241 . Review of applications will begin on October 4, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. The salary for this position will range from $105,000 to $135,000 and will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training . For further information, we can be reached via email at cssearch@pomona.edu."
    },
    "https://www.pomona.edu/administration/academic-dean/faculty-jobs#assistant-professor-of-dance": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Dance",
      "description": "Job Description The Department of Dance at Pomona College invites applications for a tenure-track position in Dance Studies, with a strong emphasis in dance history, at the rank of Assistant Professor, to begin July 1, 2027. The successful candidate will teach courses in dance history and cultural studies. Courses we expect the candidate to teach are Dance 138: Concert Dance on the Global Stage, Dance 135: Traditions of World Dance, and First-Year Seminar in Critical Thinking and Writing (ID1). The department will support the development of new courses in the candidate’s research field. The teaching load is four courses per year. The candidate will be expected to provide leadership and vision for the study of dance history and cultural studies within the Department and to expand the Department’s participation in interdisciplinary work across the Claremont Colleges. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. by the time of appointment and demonstrate specialization in one or more areas of dance history and/or cultural studies. Candidates should have college teaching experience beyond graduate assistantships, along with evidence of emerging or established scholarly research and/or professional performing experience. Application A complete application will include: (a) a letter of application; (b) a curriculum vitae; (c) graduate academic transcript(s); (d) three brief statements—one addressing teaching philosophy and experience, one addressing scholarship and/or creative research including future directions, and one addressing demonstrated ability to mentor a diverse student body; and (e) three letters of recommendation. Please submit all materials to Academic Jobs Online #32244 . The application site will prompt you to enter your recommenders’ contact information so they can submit their letters directly. Review of applications will begin December 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. For further information about the Department of Dance, please visit the Dance Department , or contact John Pennington, Chair of the Search Committee and Chair of the Department of Dance, at john.pennington@pomona.edu . Pomona College offers a robust compensation package. The salary range for this position is $95,000-$115,000 and will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training . Equal Opportunity As an equal opportunity workplace, Pomona College is committed to fairness regardless of race, color, ancestry, religion, sex, national origin, sexual orientation, age, citizenship, ability, gender identity, or veteran status. Through its Strategic Vision, Pomona College remains steadfast in its goal of creating workplace environments where all can flourish. The Department of Dance welcomes applications from scholars and artists who are ready to help expand equitable access to education and opportunity in the liberal arts."
    },
    "https://www.pomona.edu/administration/academic-dean/faculty-jobs#assistant-professor-of-english": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of English",
      "description": "The English Department at Pomona College invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professor to begin Fall 2027. At a moment when the department is in conversation about diversifying the major as well as building on its current strengths, we seek a colleague whose research focuses on US-Latinx literature and culture. We are open to various areas of expertise. Transnational, hemispheric, and/or comparative approaches welcome. Areas of interest may include but are not limited to: decolonial solidarity frameworks, environmental and digital humanities, migration, border and critical refugee studies, gender and sexuality, performance, aesthetics, global South, and considerations of the more-than-human. We seek to attract, develop, and retain faculty that contribute to the cultural, linguistic, experiential, and diverse curricular offerings of the department. The teaching load at Pomona College is two courses per semester. Faculty are expected to teach across all levels of the undergraduate curriculum and are eligible for generous teaching and research grants. Candidates should have Ph.D. in hand at time of appointment. Complete application includes: (a) cover letter, (b) curriculum vitae, (c) graduate transcripts, (d) three brief, 1-page statements – one addressing teaching philosophy and experience, one addressing scholarship and future research directions, and one addressing demonstrated ability to mentor a diverse student body (e) three letters of recommendations (f) writing sample 20pp. Please apply through Academic Jobs Online #32246 . Review of applications begins October 15, 2026. Pomona College is a highly selective private liberal arts college with an enrollment of approximately 1750 students, all undergraduates, and a student-faculty ratio of eight to one. The College and the Department support equal access to higher education and value working in a richly diverse environment. Of the students admitted into the Class of 2024, 58.8% self-identify as domestic students of color, 13.6% international and 20.7% are first generation college students. The successful candidate will have the ability to teach and mentor exceptional students of diverse backgrounds–utilizing pedagogical practices that are rigorous, inclusive, and which promote academic equity. Pomona is the founding member of The Claremont Colleges, a unique constellation of five small undergraduate colleges and two graduate institutions located in beautiful Southern California near the foothills of the San Gabriel mountains, about 35 miles east of Los Angeles. Students of the Claremont Colleges can cross-register for courses at any of the colleges and have access to a shared library system containing over 2 million volumes. Pomona students thus enjoy the advantages of personalized instruction and close faculty-student interaction in a small liberal arts college setting, combined with the intellectual and extracurricular resources typically found at much larger universities. Pomona College offers robust compensation. The salary for this position will range from $105,000 to $125,000 and will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. For any questions, please contact Dr. Cherene Sherrard-Johnson, Search Committee Chair and Department Chair, cherene.sherrard-johnson@pomona.edu ."
    },
    "https://www.pomona.edu/administration/academic-dean/faculty-jobs#assistant-professor-of-environmental-analysis-critical-geographer": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Environmental Analysis (Critical Geographer)",
      "description": "Pomona College invites applications for a tenure‑track Assistant Professor of Environmental Analysis (Critical Geography) specializing in critical geography, broadly conceived, beginning July 1, 2027. We seek a scholar‑teacher committed to excellent undergraduate education whose research and teaching examine relationships among space, environment, power, and social inequality, including social‑scientific approaches to sustainability in urban and built environments. The successful candidate will: Teach EA010 (Introduction to Environmental Analysis) and contribute to the core curriculum through critical perspectives on space, environment, and society. Teach additional undergraduate courses at the intermediate and advanced levels in critical geography and related areas, which may include human–environment relations, political ecology, or social dimensions of urban and regional geographies. Teach and develop courses that integrate geospatial technologies and spatial analysis, such as ArcGIS, QGIS, and related tools, with attention to social, political, and environmental contexts. Maintain an active, peer‑reviewed program of scholarship. Mentor undergraduate research and supervise senior thesis projects. Contribute, as appropriate, to interdisciplinary programs across Pomona College and the Claremont Colleges. Qualifications Ph.D. in Geography or a related field by the time of appointment. Applicants who are ABD and expect to complete all degree requirements by Dec. 2027 are encouraged to apply. Expertise in GIS and spatial methodologies, with the ability to teach these tools in ways that foreground social, environmental, and ethical considerations. Evidence of scholarly contributions consistent with tenure expectations. Demonstrated commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion in teaching, scholarship, and mentoring. Please submit your application online at Academic Jobs Online #32245 and include: (a) cover letter, (b) curriculum vitae, (c) three 1-2 page statements – one describing teaching philosophy and experience, one describing research including future directions, and one describing a demonstrated ability to mentor a diverse student body (see Preparing your Equity and Inclusion Statement ), and (d) summary of teaching evaluations. You must also provide three letters of recommendation: the application site will prompt you to enter your recommenders’ contact information. Candidates who proceed to the next round will be asked for a sample of scholarly work such as an article-length paper or its equivalent. Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Pomona College offers a robust compensation package. The salary range for this position is $100,000 - $120,000. Pomona College, the founding member of the Claremont Colleges, is a highly selective liberal arts college attracting a diverse community of approximately 1700 students. It is part of the Claremont Consortium, which includes the five Claremont Colleges, the Claremont Graduate University, and the Keck Graduate Institute. For further information, please contact Marc Los Huertos, marc.loshuertos@pomona.edu , chair of the search committee."
    },
    "https://www.pomona.edu/administration/academic-dean/faculty-jobs#assistant-professor-of-linguistics-and-cognitive-science": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Linguistics and Cognitive Science",
      "description": "The Department of Linguistics and Cognitive Science at Pomona College invites applications for two tenure-track Assistant Professor positions in Cognitive Science to begin Fall 2027. We seek candidates in any area of specialization within Cognitive Science that helps to inform theory, methods, and/or modeling in cognition, with the goal of uniquely expanding upon the existing strengths of the current Cognitive Science faculty ( Lise Abrams and Megan Zirnstein ). The Cognitive Science major is diverse, interdisciplinary, and rapidly growing in student interest. Therefore, we are particularly eager to see applications from an equally diverse pool of candidates who are outstanding scholars, dedicated educators, and enthusiastic about contributing to a vibrant, liberal arts community. Candidates will provide evidence of a demonstrated commitment to inclusive excellence in teaching and mentoring of undergraduates, a productive record of research, and an ability to involve undergraduates in research and advise thesis projects. Professors are expected to maintain an active research program along with a teaching load of two courses per semester. Courses to be taught include Introduction to Cognitive Science as well as new courses at the intermediate and advanced levels within the candidate's area(s) of expertise, which will contribute to a core set of departmental classes for the Cognitive Science major. The major is truly interdisciplinary, allowing students to take not only Cognitive Science courses but also Linguistics courses within the department and courses in other departments such as Philosophy, Computer Science, and Music. Candidates must have earned a Ph.D. by the start of the position, which is July 1, 2027. Please submit your application online at Academic Jobs Online #32240 and include: (a) cover letter, (b) curriculum vitae, (c) three 1-2 page statements – one describing teaching philosophy and experience, one describing research including future directions, and one describing a demonstrated ability to mentor a diverse student body (see Preparing your Equity and Inclusion Statement ), (d) summary of teaching evaluations, and (e) up to three samples of published work or research in progress. You must also provide three letters of recommendation: the application site will prompt you to enter your recommenders’ contact information. Review of applications will begin on September 1, 2026, and will continue until the two positions are filled. Pomona College offers robust compensation. The salary for this position will range from $105,000 to $130,000 and will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training . Pomona College, the founding member of the Claremont Colleges, is a highly selective liberal arts college attracting a diverse community of approximately 1700 students. It is part of the Claremont Consortium, which includes the five Claremont Colleges, the Claremont Graduate University, and the Keck Graduate Institute. For further information, please contact Dr. Lise Abrams, lise.abrams@pomona.edu , chair of the search committee."
    },
    "https://www.pomona.edu/administration/academic-dean/faculty-jobs#assistant-professor-of-politics-race-and-ethnic-politics": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Politics (Race and Ethnic Politics)",
      "description": "The Department of Politics at Pomona College invites applications for an Assistant Professor in American Politics beginning Fall 2027. We are seeking candidates with expertise in Race and Ethnic Politics (REP) with a preference for candidates whose research and teaching interests complement those of the current faculty or who can contribute to expanding the curriculum. The successful candidate will regularly teach Introduction to American Politics and additional courses of their own design in their fields of expertise. We are especially looking for candidates who wish to advise and mentor undergraduates and whose teaching demonstrates commitment to inclusive excellence. The teaching load is two courses per semester, and professors are expected to maintain an active research program as well as participate in department and faculty governance. Please submit your application online to Academic Jobs Online #32247 and include: (a) cover letter, (b) curriculum vitae, (c) two, 1-2 page statements - one describing teaching philosophy and experience with inclusive pedagogy and one describing research, (d) teaching evaluations, (e) two sample syllabi, and (f) and one writing sample of published work or research in progress. You must also provide three letters of recommendation: the application site will prompt you to enter your recommenders’ contact information. The institution : Pomona College is a highly selective liberal arts college with an enrollment of approximately 1700 students, all undergraduates. We seek to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty who are committed to working with undergraduates. We welcome applications from candidates who can demonstrate a commitment to creating inclusive learning in their classrooms. Pomona College is the founding member of the Claremont Colleges, which also include Claremont McKenna, Scripps, Pitzer, Harvey Mudd, Claremont Graduate University, and Keck Graduate Institute. Collectively, the Claremont Colleges constitute an academic community of approximately 8,000 students. As a result, Pomona students enjoy both the personalized instruction and close faculty-student interactions afforded by a small liberal arts college as well as the intellectual and extracurricular resources typically found at much larger universities. Pomona College is in Claremont, which is located east of Los Angeles and is accessible to the region’s major cities. Qualifications : PhD in Political Science. Advanced doctoral students will also be considered. Application procedures : Review of applications will begin on September 30, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Only complete applications will be given full consideration. Pomona College offers a competitive compensation package commensurate with experience. The salary range for this position is $105,000-$125,000; compensation is based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training . Tenure-track faculty also receive a full year pre-tenure paid sabbatical. Please contact the department chair, Professor Tom Le, tom.le@pomona.edu (tel. (909) 607-8202), with any questions you may have."
    },
    "https://www.pomona.edu/administration/academic-dean/faculty-jobs#assistant-professor-of-psychological-science-and-neuroscience": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Psychological Science and Neuroscience",
      "description": "The Departments of Psychological Science and Neuroscience seek candidates with a demonstrated commitment to teaching and the ability to establish an active research program that involves undergraduates. The position (75% appointment in Psychological Science and 25% appointment in Neuroscience) will begin in July 2027. The candidate is expected to teach two courses per semester, in addition to supervising senior theses. Courses may include introductory psychology and/or human neuroscience, as well as courses (elective, lab, or seminar) in the candidate’s area of research expertise. The successful candidate will have a productive research program examining the neural and/or physiological mechanisms of psychological phenomena. Pomona College is a selective liberal arts college, known for small classes, a challenging curriculum, and close relationships between students and faculty. The College has a generous pre-tenure sabbatical and offers internal support for faculty research, as well as professional development opportunities for faculty at all stages of their careers. The Departments of Psychological Science and Neuroscience are strongly committed to inclusive excellence in teaching and mentoring a diverse student body. Candidates should have a Ph.D. in Psychology, Neuroscience, or a related field. Please submit a dossier including (a) a cover letter, (b) curriculum vitae, (c) three brief statements – one addressing teaching philosophy and experience; one addressing scholarship, including future directions; and one addressing the candidate’s ability to mentor a diverse student body, (d) up to three representative reprints or pre-prints, and (e) three letters of recommendation. Candidates are welcome but not required to submit teaching evaluations (comprehensive information rather than selected comments are preferred). Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. All dossiers will be submitted to Academic Jobs Online #32248 . Questions should be referred to Professor Sara Masland ( Sara.Masland@pomona.edu ). Pomona College offers a robust compensation and benefits package. The salary range for this position is $105,000-130,000. Compensation is based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training."
    },
    "https://www.pomona.edu/administration/academic-dean/faculty-jobs#assistant-professor-of-sociology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Sociology",
      "description": "The Department of Sociology at Pomona College invites applications for an Assistant Professor to begin Fall 2027. Areas of specialization are open, but preference will be given to candidates who can teach sociology electives currently not offered by the Claremont Colleges. Candidates are expected to rotate into teaching introduction to sociology and/or the senior seminar. We also welcome candidates who can teach qualitative research methods and/or classical sociological theory. The teaching load is two courses per semester. The candidate must have demonstrated the ability to maintain an active, high-quality research program and the highest standards of excellence in teaching. For more information about the department, please visit our sociology department website. Pomona College is the founding member of the Claremont Colleges (Claremont Graduate University, Claremont McKenna, Harvey Mudd, Pitzer, Pomona, and Scripps), private, liberal arts colleges located in the Los Angeles metropolitan area. Pomona College and the Sociology Department support equal access to higher education. Of the students admitted into the class of 2029, 55% self-identify as domestic students of color, 15% international and 24% are first generation college students. The successful candidate will have demonstrated ability or potential to teach and mentor exceptional students of diverse backgrounds—utilizing pedagogical practices that are rigorous, inclusive, and which promote academic equity. Candidates must have earned a Ph.D. in sociology by the start of the position, which is July 1, 2027. Applications should include a cover letter; curriculum vitae; research statement; teaching statement that addresses the candidate's ability to teach, mentor, and inspire students representing a broad range of backgrounds ; evidence of teaching effectiveness; and a writing sample. Please also provide three letters of recommendation: the application site will prompt you to enter your recommenders’ contact information. Complete applications will be reviewed beginning September 9, 2026. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Dossiers should be submitted to Academic Jobs Online #32242 . Pomona College offers robust compensation. The salary for this position will range from $105,000 to $125,000 and will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training . For further information, please contact Hung Cam Thai, hung.thai@pomona.edu , chair of the search committee. As an equal opportunity workplace, Pomona College is committed to fairness regardless of race, color, ancestry, religion, sex, national origin, sexual orientation, age, citizenship, ability, gender identity or Veteran status. Through its Strategic Vision, Pomona College remains steadfast in its goal of creating workplace environments where all can flourish. The department welcomes applications from scholars who are ready to contribute to providing equitable access to education and expanding opportunities in the liberal arts and natural sciences."
    },
    "https://www.pomona.edu/administration/academic-dean/faculty-jobs#one-year-visiting-assistant-professor-of-art-ay27-28": {
      "title": "One year Visiting Assistant Professor of Art (AY27-28)",
      "description": "Job Description: The Art Department at Pomona College invites applications for a one-year, non-tenure track, Visiting Assistant Professor. The position is full-time and has a teaching load of five courses for the academic year. Each semester, the successful candidate will teach two painting courses. One will be an introductory course covering the fundamentals of oil painting; the other, an intermediate or advanced painting course, which should include a figure painting course during the year. In line with the learning outcomes of our department, these courses should address the foundational, theoretical, and critical skills associated with the creation of art. The fifth course can be from the candidate’s own area of interest and research, and could include performance, installation, multimedia, drawing, art theory, use of technology or design. The Art Department includes five faculty members, with full participation expected in department events and pedagogical functions such as exhibitions and critiques for our majors. The department has its own gallery, offering abundant opportunities for visiting artists to exhibit. Our students come from a range of backgrounds and interests, and the preferred candidate should demonstrate a commitment to engage with our wide-ranging student body, adapting their pedagogy to reach those who are coming to an art class for the first time, while providing challenges for students with more experience. Pomona College is a selective private liberal arts college with an enrollment of approximately 1700 students, all undergraduates, and a student/faculty ratio of seven to one. Recognized as one of the nation's premier liberal arts colleges, Pomona offers instruction in all major fields of the fine arts, humanities, social sciences, and natural sciences, and is strongly committed to the value of a residential educational community. Pomona is the founding college of the seven-member Claremont Colleges, a consortium located 35 miles east of Los Angeles. Fifty-two percent of our current cohort of first-year students (class of 2023) self-identify as domestic students of color, 11% international, and 19% are first generation college students. Preference will be given to applicants with a commitment to the College's goal of improving higher education for underrepresented students. Essential Functions : The successful candidate will have demonstrated ability or potential to teach and mentor exceptional students of diverse backgrounds—utilizing pedagogical practices that are rigorous, inclusive, and which promote academic equity. Additional responsibilities include managing the painting studio: ordering and maintaining materials and supplies within the department’s budget, overseeing proper use of storage and supplies, following safety procedures for solvents and waste disposal, and keeping the studio in good condition for classes and student work. Qualifications: Education--Required: MFA in Painting/Fine Arts Teaching Experience--Required: College teaching experience To Apply: Upload a single PDF document (up to 5MB) to Workday REQ#8494 that includes: a) a letter of application, b) CV (with a portfolio URL link of your own and students’ work), c) samples of course syllabi for painting, d) statement of teaching philosophy and inclusive learning practices with a proposal for the 5th course, and e) teaching evaluations. You will be asked to provide contact information for three references. Review of completed applications will begin on November 10 and will continue until the position is filled. Please send inquiries to tricia.avant@pomona.edu . This position is not eligible for travel or moving expenses. Employment is contingent upon providing proof of the legal right to work in the United States as required by the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986 for the duration of the contract, July 1, 2027 to June 30, 2028. Salary Range: $74,000 - $81,000. Salary is commensurate with years in rank and teaching experience. As an equal opportunity workplace, Pomona College is committed to fairness regardless of race, color, ancestry, religion, sex, national origin, sexual orientation, age, citizenship, ability, gender identity or Veteran status. Through its Strategic Vision, Pomona College remains steadfast in its goal of creating workplace environments where all can flourish. The department welcomes applications from scholars who are ready to contribute to providing equitable access to education and expanding opportunities in the liberal arts and natural sciences. Requirements: Instruction begins on Monday, August 30, 2027 for the fall semester and on Tuesday, January 18th, 2028 for the spring semester. After submitting proof of work authorization and completing onboarding, Visiting Assistant Professors are expected to prepare for their classes and attend the New Faculty Orientation, held in the weeks before classes start in the fall. This non-tenure-track position is instructional and does not require student advising, faculty service or participation in faculty governance. ADA/OSHA: This job description defines the essential job duties of this position. The College expects that employees hired for this position can perform the essential functions of this job without imposing risk of substantial harm to the health or safety of themselves or others. It may also include marginal functions, generally defined within Title I of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and the Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA). Disclaimer: This job description has been designed to indicate the general nature and level of work performed by employees within this classification. It is not designed to contain or be interpreted as a comprehensive inventory of all duties, responsibilities and qualifications required of employees assigned to this position. When duties and responsibilities change and develop, the College will review this job description and make changes of business necessity."
    },
    "https://www.pomona.edu/administration/academic-dean/faculty-jobs#two-year-pomona-college-scholar-in-residence-pre-or-postdoctoral-fellowship": {
      "title": "Two-Year Pomona College Scholar-in-Residence Pre- or Postdoctoral Fellowship",
      "description": "Pomona College seeks applications for the Pomona College Scholar-in-Residence Pre- or Postdoctoral Fellowship, generously funded by Fred and Dorothy Chau. The Pomona College Scholar-in-Residence Pre- or Postdoctoral Fellowship is a two-year position that starts July 1, 2027. Fellows teach two courses per year (one each semester) and are also responsible for public presentations. The Fellowship stipend is $77,000, includes $3000 in research or conference funds each year of the fellowship, and eligibility for some benefits, such as health and dental insurance. The Pomona College Scholar-in-Residence Program welcomes applications from emerging scholars in the humanities and social sciences (specifically, in disciplines represented at Pomona). For this year’s call, the College is especially interested in scholars who are poised to make critical interventions in humanities and social science disciplines by offering courses that expand curricula and whose research expertise includes perspectives from historically marginalized communities. At Pomona College, cultivating an inclusive classroom through discussions, experimentation, creative expression, dialogue, experiential learning, and methodological exploration, is a criterion for all reviews of teaching. Located in southern California, Pomona College is a selective liberal arts college with an enrollment of approximately 1700 undergraduate students and is a founding member of the Claremont Colleges, which include Claremont McKenna, Scripps, Pitzer, Harvey Mudd, Keck Graduate Institute and Claremont Graduate University. Collectively, The Claremont Colleges constitute an academic community of 9,000 students. As a result, Pomona students enjoy both the personalized instruction and close faculty-student interactions afforded by a small liberal arts college as well as the intellectual and extracurricular resources typically found at larger universities. In collaboration with the Claremont Colleges Consortium, Pomona College offers a variety of professional development, mentorship, and networking opportunities for fellows. You may find more information, including eligibility requirements on our Pomona College Scholar-in-Residence web page . Please submit a cover letter (2-3pgs) that describes 1) teaching experience, interest, and potential as well as research/dissertation progress; 2) a CV; 3) three letters of recommendation; and 4) a writing sample (12-20 pp) by December 1, 2026 to Academic Jobs Online #32966 . Review of all applications will begin after December 1. Pomona College departments will submit their nominations between January 17-24, 2026 and on-campus interviews for three finalists will begin in February. Employment is contingent upon providing proof of the legal right to work in the United States as required by the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986 for the duration of the contract, July 1, 2027 until June 30, 2029."
    }
  },
  "rows": {
